A corresponding zone is the zone that is mirrored to any one zone, with the exception of the center zone (as an example, the bottom left corner's corresponding zone is the top right corner, and a2's corresponding zone is i8.) The center zone has no corresponding zone.

There are no zone numbers.

### Movement

Monsters have the movement abilities of what their grade provides + what is in their card effects. "Capturing" a piece as in other board games translates to attacking in this game, except you do not move to that space. A monster cannot attack if it has moved and vice versa. A monster moving via its own card effect is equivalent to a monster moving via its innate ability.

For the following list, it will be formatted as such:

{grade(type set)} - {corresponding piece present in existing games, or a movement definition}

Type sets are as follows:

Type A - {Fire, Water, Ice, Earth, Air}
Type B - {Magic, Deity, Light, Seraphim}
Type C - {Dark, Dragon, Tech, Demon}

For cards with dual typing spanning two or more type groups, the controller chooses its primary type on summon, determining its type group. Monsters without a valid type (no fundamental type) inherit the movement of B-S(C).

For any given Shogi Format game, players can either choose Shogi Format: Chess, where all pieces have relatively great mobility, making the board fluid, or Shogi Format: Shogi, where all pieces have relatively bad mobility, making coordination important.

